export const FS6={title:'Forensic anthropology and odontology',hook:'When remains are found as bones, the skeleton becomes the witness. A forensic anthropologist can estimate sex, age, stature and ancestry, identify injuries and help determine how long remains have been exposed. Forensic anthropology is 10% of the test, and its methods often appear in case studies involving skeletal or decomposed remains.',objectives:['Distinguish human from nonhuman bone and describe taphonomy.','Estimate sex, age and stature from skeletal features.','Distinguish antemortem, perimortem and postmortem trauma.','Explain the role of forensic odontology in identification.'],sections:[
['What forensic anthropologists do',[
'**Forensic anthropologists** apply knowledge of the human skeleton to legal investigations. They help **recover** remains, determine whether bones are **human**, estimate the **number of individuals**, build a **biological profile** (sex, age, stature and ancestry), identify **trauma** and **pathology** and estimate time since death in skeletal cases.',
'The adult human skeleton has **206 bones**. Nonhuman bones may be distinguished by size, shape, density and microscopic structure. **Taphonomy** is the study of what happens to remains after death—decomposition, animal scavenging, weathering, burial and movement by water—which affects how bones are interpreted.',
'Anthropologists work closely with pathologists, odontologists and DNA analysts to identify remains.',
order('Anthropological analysis',['Recover remains carefully','Determine whether bones are human','Estimate number of individuals','Build the biological profile','Identify trauma and pathology','Assess taphonomy and time since death'])]],
['Estimating sex',[
'In adults, the **pelvis** is the most reliable indicator of sex. Female pelves are adapted for childbirth: a **wider subpubic angle** (greater than about 90°), a **wider greater sciatic notch**, a broader, more circular **pelvic inlet** and a more outward-flaring shape. Male pelves have a narrower subpubic angle and sciatic notch.',
'The **skull** also helps: male skulls often have **larger mastoid processes**, more prominent **brow ridges**, a more pronounced **nuchal crest** and a **squarer chin**; female skulls tend to be smoother with a more pointed chin. Estimates are given as probable rather than certain, and sex is difficult to estimate reliably in children before puberty.',
'Combining several features improves accuracy.',
"Skeletal sex estimation is usually reported as “probable female” or “probable male” with a level of confidence, reflecting overlap among individuals and populations.",
sort('Typically female or male skeletal trait?',['Typically female','Typically male'],[['Subpubic angle greater than 90°',0],['Prominent brow ridges',1],['Wide greater sciatic notch',0],['Large mastoid process',1],['Pointed chin',0],['Square chin',1]])]],
['Estimating age',[
'In **children and adolescents**, age is estimated from **dental development** (tooth formation and eruption) and **epiphyseal fusion**—growth plates at the ends of long bones fuse in a predictable sequence through the teens and twenties. In **adults**, anthropologists examine degenerative changes in the **pubic symphysis**, the **auricular surface** of the pelvis, the **sternal ends of the ribs** and, less reliably, **cranial suture** closure.',
'Adult age estimates have wider ranges because aging varies among individuals. Arthritis and bone density changes add information in older adults.',
'A case might describe unfused growth plates (suggesting a teen or young adult) or advanced joint degeneration (suggesting an older adult).',
match('Feature and age information',[['Tooth eruption','Children’s age'],['Epiphyseal fusion','Adolescents and young adults'],['Pubic symphysis','Adult age changes'],['Sternal rib ends','Adult age changes'],['Cranial sutures','Less reliable adult indicator'],['Arthritis','Often older adults']])]],
['Estimating stature and ancestry',[
'**Stature** (living height) is estimated from the lengths of **long bones**, especially the **femur**, using **regression equations** developed from reference populations. The femur is about one-quarter of a person’s height as a rough guide. Equations differ by sex and population, so anthropologists give a height **range**.',
'**Ancestry** estimation uses skeletal traits that vary in frequency among populations, but it is complex and controversial. Human variation is continuous, and modern anthropologists use statistical methods cautiously and recognize that race is largely a social category. In case studies, focus on sex, age, stature and trauma unless ancestry is clearly provided.',
'Individualizing features—healed fractures, surgical implants with serial numbers or distinctive dental work—are especially valuable for identification.',
ex('Rough stature estimate','An invented femur measures 45 cm.',['Rough rule: femur ≈ one-quarter of stature','Stature ≈ 45 × 4 = 180 cm','Convert: 180 cm ÷ 2.54 ≈ 71 inches (about 5 ft 11 in)','Real cases use sex- and population-specific regression equations and give a range'],'About 180 cm as a rough estimate, refined with formal equations (invented for practice).')]],
['Skeletal trauma and pathology',[
'Anthropologists classify trauma by timing. **Antemortem** trauma occurred before death and shows **healing** (rounded edges, new bone). **Perimortem** trauma occurred at or around the time of death; living (“green”) bone bends and fractures with sharp edges and no healing. **Postmortem** damage happens after death; dry bone breaks with square or jagged edges and often a different color at the break.',
'Trauma is also classified by mechanism: **blunt force** (depressed or radiating fractures), **sharp force** (cut marks) and **projectile** (gunshot entrance with inward beveling and exit with outward beveling on the skull). Pathologies such as healed fractures, arthritis or infection add to the biological profile.',
'Distinguishing perimortem from postmortem damage is crucial for determining whether injuries relate to death.',
"Bones can also show evidence of medical treatment, such as surgical plates or joint replacements, some of which carry serial numbers that can be traced to the patient.",
sort('Timing of skeletal trauma',['Antemortem','Perimortem','Postmortem'],[['Rounded edges with new bone formation',0],['Fresh fracture with sharp edges and no healing',1],['Dry-bone break with a different color at the edges',2],['Healed childhood fracture',0],['Fracture consistent with the fatal injury',1],['Damage from scavenging animals after death',2]])]],
['Forensic odontology',[
'**Forensic odontologists** use **teeth** and **dental records** to identify people, especially when bodies are badly decomposed, burned or fragmented. Teeth are the hardest substances in the body and survive extreme conditions. Comparing postmortem dental X-rays and charts with antemortem records—fillings, crowns, missing teeth and root shapes—can provide positive identification.',
'Odontologists also estimate age from dental development and wear and may analyze bite marks, although bite mark comparison has been criticized for limited scientific reliability.',
'Dental identification is widely used in mass fatality incidents.',
chk('Why are teeth valuable for identification?',['They are very durable and can be compared with dental records','They always contain the person’s name','They never change during life','They are the softest tissue'],'Teeth survive extreme conditions.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Skeletal remains',{a:{text:'Invented remains show a wide subpubic angle, unfused growth plates at the knee and a 43 cm femur. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the likely biological profile?',choices:[['Probable female, adolescent or young adult, about 170 cm tall','b','Correct.',true],['Probable male, older adult, about 140 cm','b','Check each feature.',false]]},b:{text:'A skull fracture has sharp edges and no healing. What does this suggest?',choices:[['Perimortem trauma, possibly related to death','c','Correct.',true],['Antemortem trauma that healed','c','Healing would show rounded edges.',false]]},c:{text:'You recommend dental comparison and DNA testing for identification.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Forensic anthropologists recover remains, determine if bones are human, estimate number of individuals and build a biological profile; the adult skeleton has 206 bones; taphonomy explains postmortem changes.','Sex: pelvis most reliable (female wider subpubic angle and sciatic notch), skull features; age: dental eruption and epiphyseal fusion in youth, pubic symphysis and rib ends in adults; stature from long bones (femur ≈ one-quarter of height) with equations; ancestry estimated cautiously.','Antemortem trauma shows healing, perimortem shows fresh fractures without healing, postmortem shows dry-bone breaks; forensic odontology identifies people by comparing dental records.'],terms:[['Biological profile','Estimates of sex, age, stature and ancestry from remains.'],['Perimortem','At or around the time of death.'],['Taphonomy','The study of what happens to remains after death.']],mcqs:[q('Which bone is most reliable for estimating sex in adults?','Pelvis','Femur','Radius','Clavicle','Childbirth adaptations.'),q('A subpubic angle greater than 90° suggests:','Female','Male','Child','Nonhuman','Wider angle.'),q('Which feature helps estimate age in adolescents?','Epiphyseal fusion','Mastoid size','Brow ridges','Bite marks','Growth plates.'),q('Which bone is often used to estimate stature?','Femur','Skull','Rib','Vertebra','Long bones.'),q('Healing bone with rounded edges indicates:','Antemortem trauma','Perimortem trauma','Postmortem damage','No trauma','Healing occurred.'),q('How many bones are in the adult human skeleton?','206','300','150','250','Adult count.'),q('Forensic odontologists primarily use:','Teeth and dental records','Insects','Fingerprints','Blood typing','Dental identification.'),q('The study of what happens to remains after death is:','Taphonomy','Toxicology','Serology','Odontology','Postmortem processes.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a forensic anthropologist builds a biological profile from skeletal remains and interprets trauma.',rubric:[['Human and taphonomy',['human','206','number of individuals','taphonomy','recover']],['Sex and age',['pelvis','subpubic','sciatic','skull','epiphyseal','pubic symphysis','teeth']],['Stature and ancestry',['femur','long bone','regression','range','ancestry','caution']],['Trauma and identification',['antemortem','perimortem','postmortem','healing','blunt','sharp','odontology','dna']]],model:'A forensic anthropologist first recovers the remains carefully, confirms they are human and estimates the number of individuals, considering taphonomic processes such as scavenging and weathering that may have moved or damaged bones. To estimate sex, the anthropologist examines the pelvis, the most reliable indicator: a wide subpubic angle greater than about 90 degrees, a wide greater sciatic notch and a broad pelvic inlet suggest a female, while narrower features suggest a male; skull features such as mastoid size, brow ridges and chin shape provide supporting evidence. Age is estimated in young people from dental development and epiphyseal fusion of growth plates, and in adults from changes in the pubic symphysis, auricular surface and sternal rib ends, giving wider ranges for older adults. Stature is estimated from long bones, especially the femur, using sex- and population-specific regression equations, with the femur roughly one-quarter of living height as a rough check, and ancestry is estimated cautiously because human variation is continuous. To interpret trauma, the anthropologist distinguishes antemortem injuries, which show healing; perimortem injuries, which show fresh fractures in living bone without healing and may relate to death; and postmortem damage, which shows dry-bone breaks. Mechanism—blunt, sharp or projectile—is identified from fracture patterns and cut marks. Finally, the profile and any individualizing features, such as healed fractures or implants, guide identification through dental comparison by a forensic odontologist and DNA testing.'},sources:[RULES,OS_AP,NIJ]};

export const FS7={title:'Forensic entomology',hook:'Insects often find a body within minutes and colonize it in a predictable order. Because insect development depends on temperature, the age of the oldest larvae can reveal the minimum time since death—even when other indicators no longer work. Forensic entomology is 10% of the test, and insect evidence may appear in case studies of remains found outdoors.',objectives:['Describe the role of forensic entomology in estimating time since death.','Explain the blowfly life cycle and where eggs are laid.','Calculate development using accumulated degree days.','Describe insect succession and factors that affect colonization.'],sections:[
['Why insects matter',[
'**Forensic entomology** uses insects and other arthropods to answer legal questions, most often the **postmortem interval (PMI)**—the time since death. After about **three days**, traditional indicators such as body temperature and rigor become less useful, while insect evidence becomes increasingly valuable.',
'Insects can also show whether a body was **moved** (insects not native to the location), whether wounds were present (early colonization at wound sites) and sometimes whether drugs were present (**entomotoxicology**, testing larvae for drugs).',
'Entomologists collect insects at different life stages from the body and surroundings, record **temperatures** and preserve or rear specimens to identify species and age.',
"Entomologists collect live specimens to rear to adulthood, which confirms species identification, and preserve others immediately to record their exact developmental stage at the time of collection. They also record ambient, ground and maggot-mass temperatures.",
chk('What is the postmortem interval?',['The time since death','The time from injury to death','The length of an autopsy','The time insects take to hatch'],'Entomology estimates a minimum PMI.')]],
['Blowflies and where eggs are laid',[
'**Blowflies** (family Calliphoridae) are usually the **first insects** to arrive, often within minutes to hours after death when conditions allow. Females lay eggs in **moist, protected areas**: **natural openings** (eyes, nose, mouth, ears) and **wounds**. Heavy early insect activity in an unusual location can therefore suggest an injury there.',
'**Flesh flies** (Sarcophagidae) also arrive early and deposit live larvae rather than eggs. Blowflies are less active at night and in cold or rainy weather, which can delay colonization.',
'The location of egg masses and early larvae is a common test question.',
sort('Typical early egg-laying site?',['Common early site','Less likely early site'],[['A wound',0],['The nose and mouth',0],['The eyes',0],['Dry skin on the back of the hand far from openings',1],['The ears',0]])]],
['The blowfly life cycle',[
'Blowflies undergo **complete metamorphosis**: **egg** → **larva** (maggot) through **three instars** (growth stages separated by molts) → **pupa** (inside a hardened puparium) → **adult**. Larvae feed in masses that generate heat, which can speed development. Mature third-instar larvae stop feeding and **migrate** away from the body to pupate.',
'Because each stage takes a predictable amount of time at a given temperature, identifying the **oldest stage present** gives a **minimum PMI**: the body must have been dead at least long enough for that stage to develop.',
'Empty pupal cases indicate adults have already emerged, suggesting a longer interval.',
"Because maggot masses generate their own heat, larvae in large masses may develop faster than air temperatures alone would predict. Entomologists measure mass temperatures and consider this effect when estimating development time.",
order('Blowfly life cycle',['Egg','First-instar larva','Second-instar larva','Third-instar larva','Pupa','Adult fly'])]],
['Temperature and accumulated degree days',[
'Insect development depends on **temperature**: warmer conditions speed growth; cold slows or stops it. Entomologists use **accumulated degree hours (ADH)** or **accumulated degree days (ADD)**—the amount of heat energy above a species’ **minimum developmental temperature** needed to reach each stage.',
'To estimate time, they obtain temperature records (often from a nearby weather station, corrected for the scene) and count backward until the accumulated heat matches what the oldest specimens needed.',
"Accurate temperature data are essential. Investigators may record scene temperatures for several days after discovery and compare them with nearby weather station records to correct for differences.",
ex('Accumulated degree days','An invented species needs 200 ADD above a 10 °C base to reach the third instar. The scene averaged 20 °C per day.',['Daily degree days = 20 − 10 = 10 ADD per day','Days needed = 200 ÷ 10 = 20 days','Oldest larvae are third instar','Minimum PMI is about 20 days'],'About 20 days minimum PMI (data invented for practice).')]],
['Insect succession',[
'Different insects arrive in a predictable **succession** as decomposition progresses. **Blowflies and flesh flies** arrive first during the fresh and bloat stages. **Predatory beetles** (such as rove beetles) and **wasps** arrive to feed on fly larvae. Later, **hide beetles (Dermestidae)** and other species feed on dried tissue and skin during advanced decay and the dry stage.',
'Succession patterns vary with **geography**, **season**, **habitat** (sun vs shade, urban vs rural) and whether the body is **indoors**, **buried**, **wrapped** or in **water**. Local reference data are needed for accurate estimates.',
'Succession helps estimate longer intervals when flies have completed several generations.',
match('Insect and typical role',[['Blowfly','First arrival; eggs in openings and wounds'],['Flesh fly','Early arrival; deposits live larvae'],['Rove beetle','Predator of fly larvae'],['Hide beetle (Dermestidae)','Feeds on dried tissue later'],['Wasp','Predator or parasite of larvae'],['Puparium','Hardened case around the pupa']])]],
['Factors affecting colonization',[
'Several factors can delay or alter insect activity: **cold temperatures**, **rain**, **darkness**, **burial**, **wrapping**, **indoor locations** with closed windows and **submersion** in water. Chemicals such as insecticides or certain drugs in the body can also affect development.',
'Entomologists document these conditions and adjust estimates accordingly. Because colonization may not begin immediately, entomological estimates are typically reported as a **minimum PMI** rather than an exact time of death.',
'In a case study, note the environment, season and any barriers to insect access when interpreting insect evidence.',
"In a case study, insect evidence is most useful when combined with other indicators. For example, a body with fresh-stage features and only first-instar larvae suggests a short interval, while abundant pupae and advanced decomposition suggest weeks.",
sort('Would it delay insect colonization?',['Delays','Does not delay'],[['Body wrapped tightly in plastic',0],['Warm, sunny summer day',1],['Burial',0],['Cold, rainy weather',0],['Open wounds in a warm outdoor setting',1],['Closed indoor room in winter',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Outdoor remains',{a:{text:'An invented case: remains found outdoors in summer with many third-instar larvae and no pupae, clustered at the face and a wound on the arm. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does larval location suggest?',choices:[['Colonization at natural openings and a wound, consistent with an injury on the arm','b','Correct.',true],['The arm wound happened after insects arrived','b','Early colonization occurs at existing wounds.',false]]},b:{text:'With no pupae present, how is the PMI reported?',choices:[['A minimum PMI based on time needed to reach the third instar at local temperatures','c','Correct.',true],['An exact minute of death','c','Entomology gives minimum estimates.',false]]},c:{text:'You combine the entomology estimate with scene information to state a range.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Forensic entomology estimates the postmortem interval, especially after about three days, and can show movement of a body, wound sites and drugs (entomotoxicology).','Blowflies arrive first and lay eggs in natural openings and wounds; complete metamorphosis: egg, three larval instars, pupa, adult; the oldest stage gives a minimum PMI.','Development depends on temperature measured as accumulated degree hours or days above a base temperature; succession moves from flies to predatory and hide beetles; cold, rain, burial, wrapping and indoor settings delay colonization.'],terms:[['Postmortem interval','The time since death.'],['Instar','A larval growth stage between molts.'],['Accumulated degree days','Heat units above a base temperature needed for insect development.']],mcqs:[q('Where are blowfly eggs usually found on a dead body?','Close to a wound or natural orifice','Under any covering such as clothing, leaves, etc.','On the chest or in a hairy body area','Under the body close to the ground','HOSA sample question (Bell).'),q('Which insects usually arrive first?','Blowflies','Hide beetles','Ants only','Moths','Calliphoridae.'),q('How many larval instars does a blowfly have?','Three','One','Five','Ten','Then pupa.'),q('The oldest insect stage present gives a:','Minimum postmortem interval','Maximum postmortem interval','Cause of death','Manner of death','Lower limit.'),q('If a species needs 300 ADD and the scene provides 15 ADD per day, how many days are needed?','20','15','45','300','300 ÷ 15.'),q('Testing larvae for drugs is called:','Entomotoxicology','Odontology','Serology','Anthropology','Insects and toxicology.'),q('Which delays insect colonization?','Burial','Warm sunny weather','Open wounds','Outdoor summer setting','Barrier to access.'),q('Hide beetles (Dermestidae) typically arrive:','Later, feeding on dried tissue','First, within minutes','Only in water','Never on human remains','Succession.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how forensic entomologists estimate the postmortem interval, including the blowfly life cycle, temperature effects and an example calculation with invented data.',rubric:[['Role',['postmortem interval','minimum','three days','moved','entomotoxicology']],['Life cycle',['blowfly','eggs','openings','wounds','instar','pupa','adult','metamorphosis']],['Temperature',['temperature','accumulated degree','base','weather station','calculation']],['Succession and factors',['succession','beetles','burial','wrapped','cold','indoor']]],model:'Forensic entomologists estimate the postmortem interval, the time since death, by determining the age of insects developing on the remains. Blowflies are usually the first to arrive, often within minutes to hours, and lay eggs in moist, protected sites such as the eyes, nose, mouth and wounds. They undergo complete metamorphosis: egg, three larval instars, pupa and adult. Because the oldest insects present could only have started developing after death, their age gives a minimum interval. Development depends on temperature, so entomologists use accumulated degree days, the heat units above a species’ minimum developmental temperature. In an invented example, a species needs 200 degree days above a 10 °C base to reach the third instar, and weather data show the scene averaged 20 °C, providing 10 degree days per day; therefore the oldest third-instar larvae needed about 20 days, so death occurred at least about 20 days earlier. Entomologists collect specimens at all stages, record scene temperatures and correct weather station data for the site. For longer intervals, they use insect succession: blowflies and flesh flies first, predatory beetles and wasps next and hide beetles feeding on dried tissue later. They also consider factors that delay colonization, such as cold, rain, darkness, burial, wrapping or an indoor setting, and report the result as a minimum postmortem interval consistent with all of the evidence.'},sources:[RULES,NIJ,NIST]};

export const FS8={title:'Blood, bloodstains and biological fluids',hook:'A small reddish stain might be blood, rust or ketchup. If it is blood, is it human? Whose is it? And what does its shape say about how it got there? Identification of blood, bloodstains and biological fluids is 10% of the test, and bloodstain patterns can help reconstruct events in a case study.',objectives:['Describe the composition of blood and its forensic significance.','Distinguish presumptive and confirmatory tests for blood and other fluids.','Classify bloodstain patterns and what they indicate.','Calculate the angle of impact of a blood drop.'],sections:[
['Blood and its components',[
'Blood consists of **plasma** (about 55%: water, proteins, nutrients) and **formed elements**: **red blood cells** (carry oxygen; no nucleus in mature cells), **white blood cells** (immune defense; contain nuclei and therefore **DNA**) and **platelets** (clotting). An average adult has about **5 liters** of blood.',
'Blood type is determined by antigens on red blood cells. Karl Landsteiner discovered the **ABO** system; the **Rh** factor adds positive or negative. ABO typing is **class evidence**—it can exclude a suspect but cannot identify an individual. **DNA** from white blood cells provides individual identification.',
'Understanding blood biology helps interpret both laboratory results and bloodstain patterns.',
match('Blood component and role',[['Plasma','Liquid portion carrying proteins and nutrients'],['Red blood cells','Carry oxygen; no nucleus when mature'],['White blood cells','Immune defense; contain DNA'],['Platelets','Clotting'],['ABO antigens','Determine blood type'],['Rh factor','Positive or negative']])]],
['Presumptive and confirmatory tests',[
'**Presumptive tests** indicate that a substance **might** be blood. The **Kastle-Meyer** test uses **phenolphthalein** and hydrogen peroxide; a **pink** color suggests blood. **Luminol** reacts with the iron in hemoglobin to produce **chemiluminescence** (a blue glow in the dark), revealing hidden or cleaned stains. **Leucomalachite green** produces a green color. Presumptive tests can give false positives with some plant materials or cleaners.',
'**Confirmatory tests** prove a substance is blood, such as crystal tests (Teichmann and Takayama) or immunological tests for human hemoglobin. The **precipitin test** determines whether blood is **human** or animal by reacting with species-specific antibodies.',
'In a sequence: presumptive → confirmatory → species → DNA.',
"Because presumptive tests can be destructive or affect later DNA testing, analysts use small portions of a stain and document every test performed.",
order('Testing a suspected bloodstain',['Document and photograph the stain','Presumptive test (Kastle-Meyer or luminol)','Confirmatory test for blood','Species test (precipitin)','DNA analysis for individual identification'])]],
['Other biological fluids',[
'**Semen** can be detected presumptively with the **acid phosphatase** test and confirmed by identifying **sperm cells** under the microscope or detecting **prostate-specific antigen (PSA, p30)**. **Saliva** is detected by **amylase** activity. **Urine** may be identified by urea or creatinine. Alternative light sources help locate many body fluid stains.',
'All of these fluids can be sources of **DNA**, linking evidence to individuals. Collection uses sterile swabs, drying and paper packaging.',
'Questions may ask which enzyme indicates saliva (amylase) or which test is presumptive for semen (acid phosphatase).',
match('Fluid and test',[['Blood','Kastle-Meyer (presumptive)'],['Hidden blood','Luminol'],['Semen','Acid phosphatase (presumptive)'],['Semen confirmation','Sperm cells or PSA'],['Saliva','Amylase'],['Human vs animal blood','Precipitin test']])]],
['Bloodstain pattern categories',[
'**Bloodstain pattern analysis (BPA)** interprets the size, shape and distribution of bloodstains. **Passive stains** form by gravity: **drips**, **pools** and **flows**. **Transfer stains** form when a bloody object contacts a surface: a **swipe** (bloody object moves across a clean surface) or **wipe** (clean object moves through existing blood), and **pattern transfers** such as a bloody shoe print.',
'**Projected or spatter stains** result from force: **impact spatter** from a blow or gunshot, **cast-off** from blood flung from a moving object, **arterial** patterns from a breached artery and **expirated** blood from the mouth or nose (often with bubbles). Spatter size relates to the force involved, although modern analysts focus on mechanism rather than older velocity labels.',
'Patterns help reconstruct positions, movements and the sequence of events.',
"Bloodstain pattern analysts document stains with photographs, scales and measurements before collecting samples, and they consider surface texture—stains on rough surfaces may be distorted compared with stains on smooth surfaces such as tile or glass.",
sort('Bloodstain category',['Passive','Transfer','Projected/spatter'],[['Drip from a wound onto the floor',0],['Bloody handprint on a wall',1],['Cast-off from a swinging object',2],['Pool beside the body',0],['Swipe from a bloody hand',1],['Impact spatter from a blow',2]])]],
['Directionality and angle of impact',[
'A blood drop striking a surface at **90°** produces a nearly **round** stain. At smaller angles, stains become **elongated**, with a **tail** pointing in the **direction of travel**. The **angle of impact** is calculated with **sin θ = width ÷ length** of the stain (excluding the tail).',
'By drawing lines through the long axes of several stains, analysts find the **area of convergence** (2D); adding angles of impact estimates the **area of origin** (3D), the approximate position of the blood source, often shown with strings or software.',
"Analysts measure many stains, not just one, because individual measurements can be affected by surface texture and drop size. The more consistent the measurements, the more confident the reconstruction.",
ex('Calculating angle of impact','An invented stain is 5 mm wide and 10 mm long.',['sin θ = width ÷ length = 5 ÷ 10 = 0.5','θ = arcsin(0.5) = 30°','The drop struck the surface at about 30°','The tail points in the direction of travel'],'About 30° (invented for practice).')]],
['Using blood evidence in a case',[
'In a case study, blood evidence may show **where** an injury occurred, whether a victim **moved** after injury (trails of drips), whether the scene was **cleaned** (luminol reactions), the **sequence** of events (overlapping stains) and the **positions** of people (voids where an object or person blocked spatter).',
'Connect blood findings to your conclusions: for example, impact spatter low on a wall and a pool near the body may support where the fatal injury occurred, while cast-off on the ceiling suggests repeated swings of an object.',
'Always describe the evidence first, then your interpretation, and avoid overstating certainty.',
chk('What does a void in a spatter pattern suggest?',['An object or person blocked the blood at the time of spatter','The blood was cleaned','The stain is not blood','The victim was not injured'],'Voids show something was in the way.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Stains at a scene',{a:{text:'An invented stain turns pink with Kastle-Meyer. (Scenario invented for practice.) What can you conclude?',choices:[['It may be blood; confirmatory and species tests are needed','b','Correct. Presumptive only.',true],['It is definitely human blood from the suspect','b','Presumptive tests can give false positives.',false]]},b:{text:'A stain is 4 mm wide and 8 mm long. What is the angle of impact?',choices:[['About 30°','c','Correct. sin θ = 0.5.',true],['About 90°','c','A 90° stain would be round.',false]]},c:{text:'You combine pattern analysis with DNA results to reconstruct events.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Blood includes plasma, red cells (no nucleus), white cells (contain DNA) and platelets; ABO typing is class evidence while DNA individualizes.','Presumptive tests (Kastle-Meyer pink, luminol glow, leucomalachite green) suggest blood; confirmatory tests prove it; precipitin determines species; acid phosphatase and PSA/sperm for semen, amylase for saliva.','Bloodstain patterns: passive (drips, pools), transfer (swipe, wipe, pattern), projected (impact, cast-off, arterial, expirated); angle of impact sin θ = width ÷ length; tails point in the direction of travel; convergence and origin reconstruct positions.'],terms:[['Presumptive test','A screening test that suggests a substance may be present.'],['Cast-off','Blood flung from a moving bloody object.'],['Angle of impact','The angle at which a blood drop strikes a surface.']],mcqs:[q('A pink color in the Kastle-Meyer test suggests:','Blood may be present','Saliva','Semen','Urine','Presumptive.'),q('Which test glows to reveal hidden blood?','Luminol','Precipitin','Amylase','Acid phosphatase','Chemiluminescence.'),q('Which test determines whether blood is human?','Precipitin test','Kastle-Meyer','Luminol','Leucomalachite green','Species.'),q('Amylase activity indicates:','Saliva','Blood','Semen','Urine','Enzyme.'),q('Which blood cells contain DNA?','White blood cells','Mature red blood cells','Platelets','Plasma','Nucleated.'),q('A stain 6 mm wide and 12 mm long struck at about:','30°','60°','90°','10°','sin θ = 0.5.'),q('Blood flung from a swinging object is:','Cast-off','A drip','A wipe','A pool','Projected.'),q('ABO blood typing is:','Class evidence','Individual evidence','A DNA test','A confirmatory test for semen','Narrows to a group.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how investigators test a suspected bloodstain and use bloodstain pattern analysis to reconstruct events, including an angle-of-impact calculation with invented data.',rubric:[['Testing',['presumptive','kastle-meyer','luminol','confirmatory','precipitin','dna']],['Blood biology',['white blood cells','dna','abo','class','individual']],['Patterns',['passive','transfer','spatter','cast-off','impact','void','direction']],['Calculation',['sin','width','length','angle','convergence','origin']]],model:'Investigators first document and photograph a suspected bloodstain, then apply a presumptive test such as Kastle-Meyer, in which phenolphthalein turns pink in the presence of hemoglobin, or luminol, which glows to reveal hidden or cleaned blood. Because these tests can give false positives, a confirmatory test proves the stain is blood, a precipitin test shows whether it is human and DNA analysis of white blood cells links it to an individual; ABO typing is only class evidence. Bloodstain pattern analysis then helps reconstruct events. Passive stains such as drips and pools show where a bleeding person stood or lay; transfer stains such as swipes, wipes and bloody shoe prints show contact and movement; and projected stains such as impact spatter, cast-off from a swinging object, arterial patterns and expirated blood show the mechanism of bloodshed. Voids in spatter show that a person or object blocked the blood. The shape of individual stains shows direction and angle: round stains struck at about 90 degrees, while elongated stains have tails pointing in the direction of travel. In an invented example, a stain 5 millimeters wide and 10 millimeters long gives sin θ = 5 ÷ 10 = 0.5, so the angle of impact is 30 degrees. Lines through several stains locate the area of convergence, and adding their angles estimates the area of origin, the approximate position of the victim when the blood was shed.'},sources:[RULES,NIST,OS_AP]};

export const FS9={title:'Techniques of DNA analysis',hook:'Every person except identical twins has a unique DNA profile, and tiny amounts of biological material—a drop of blood, a few skin cells on a handle—can produce one. DNA analysis has identified the guilty, freed the wrongly convicted and named the unidentified. Techniques of DNA analysis are 10% of the test.',objectives:['Describe DNA structure and sources of forensic DNA.','Explain the steps of STR profiling, including PCR and capillary electrophoresis.','Distinguish nuclear, mitochondrial and Y-chromosome DNA analysis.','Explain CODIS and the interpretation of DNA matches.'],sections:[
['DNA basics',[
'**DNA** (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a double helix of **nucleotides**, each with a sugar, phosphate and one of four bases: **adenine (A)**, **thymine (T)**, **guanine (G)** and **cytosine (C)**. Bases pair **A–T** and **G–C**. Most DNA is in the **nucleus**; a small circular genome is in **mitochondria**.',
'Most of the human genome is identical among people. Forensic analysis focuses on **variable regions**, especially **short tandem repeats (STRs)**—short sequences repeated a different number of times in different people. An individual inherits one copy of each STR location from each parent.',
'Understanding inheritance helps explain why relatives share more DNA and why mitochondrial and Y-chromosome DNA follow family lines.',
"Identical twins share virtually the same nuclear DNA profile, which is one of the few situations where standard STR profiles cannot distinguish individuals.",
match('DNA term and meaning',[['Nucleotide','Sugar, phosphate and base'],['Base pairing','A with T, G with C'],['Nuclear DNA','Inherited from both parents'],['Mitochondrial DNA','Inherited from the mother'],['STR','Short sequence repeated a variable number of times'],['Locus','A specific location on a chromosome']])]],
['Sources and collection',[
'DNA can be recovered from **blood**, **saliva**, **semen**, **hair roots**, **skin cells** (“touch DNA”), **bone** and **teeth**. Hair **shafts** without roots contain little nuclear DNA but may yield **mitochondrial DNA**. Bones and teeth can preserve DNA for long periods, helping identify old remains.',
'DNA evidence is highly sensitive to **contamination**. Collectors wear gloves, masks and protective clothing, use sterile swabs, change gloves between items, dry biological evidence and package it in **paper**. Elimination samples from investigators may be kept to identify contamination.',
'Degraded samples (from heat, moisture or time) may produce partial profiles.',
"Touch DNA from skin cells left on handled objects is often present in very small amounts and can be transferred indirectly—for example, from a handshake to an object the second person touches. Interpreting touch DNA therefore requires caution about how it got there.",
sort('Good source of nuclear DNA?',['Good nuclear DNA source','Limited nuclear DNA'],[['Blood with white cells',0],['Hair shaft without a root',1],['Saliva on a cup',0],['Hair with an intact root',0],['Highly degraded old bone',1],['Semen',0]])]],
['STR profiling: extraction to amplification',[
'The standard forensic method is **STR profiling**. Steps: **(1) extraction**—DNA is released from cells and purified; **(2) quantitation**—the amount of human DNA is measured; **(3) amplification** by the **polymerase chain reaction (PCR)**—millions of copies of targeted STR regions are made through repeated cycles of **denaturation** (separating strands with heat), **annealing** (primers attach) and **extension** (DNA polymerase builds new strands).',
'PCR allows analysts to work with very small samples, but it also amplifies contamination, which is why careful handling matters.',
'Fluorescent tags on primers allow the amplified STRs to be detected in the next step.',
order('PCR cycle',['Denaturation: heat separates DNA strands','Annealing: primers bind to target regions','Extension: DNA polymerase builds new strands','Repeat cycles to make millions of copies'])]],
['Separation and the DNA profile',[
'**(4) Separation and detection**: amplified STR fragments are separated by size using **capillary electrophoresis**; smaller fragments move faster. A laser detects fluorescent tags, producing an **electropherogram** with peaks representing alleles. **(5) Interpretation**: the number of repeats at each locus is recorded, creating a **DNA profile**.',
'At each locus, a person has two alleles (one from each parent). If both are the same, there is one peak (**homozygous**); if different, two peaks (**heterozygous**). Comparing profiles across many loci shows whether samples could share a source.',
'Older methods include RFLP (restriction fragment length polymorphism), which required larger samples and gel electrophoresis.',
"Mixtures—samples containing DNA from more than one person—are common and harder to interpret. Laboratories use probabilistic genotyping software to evaluate complex mixtures statistically.",
flip('DNA profiling terms',[['Capillary electrophoresis','Separates DNA fragments by size'],['Electropherogram','Graph of peaks for alleles'],['Allele','A version of a DNA sequence at a locus'],['Homozygous','Two identical alleles; one peak'],['Heterozygous','Two different alleles; two peaks'],['RFLP','Older method requiring more DNA']])]],
['Mitochondrial and Y-chromosome DNA',[
'**Mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA)** is inherited from the **mother** and exists in **many copies per cell**, so it can be recovered from hair shafts, old bones and degraded samples. However, all maternal relatives share the same mtDNA, so it cannot distinguish among them—it is **less discriminating** than nuclear STRs.',
'**Y-STR** analysis targets the **Y chromosome**, inherited from father to son. It is useful in mixtures of male and female DNA (such as sexual assault evidence) and for tracing paternal lines, but all men in a paternal line share the same Y-STR profile.',
'Choosing the right method depends on the sample and the question being asked.',
sort('Best DNA method',['Nuclear STR','Mitochondrial DNA','Y-STR'],[['Fresh bloodstain for individual identification',0],['Hair shaft without a root',1],['Mixture of male and female DNA',2],['Very old skeletal remains',1],['Saliva on a cigarette butt',0],['Tracing a paternal family line',2]])]],
['CODIS and interpreting matches',[
'The FBI’s **Combined DNA Index System (CODIS)** links local, state and national DNA databases (NDIS). It contains profiles from convicted offenders, arrestees (where authorized), crime scene evidence and missing persons. Since 2017, CODIS uses **20 core STR loci** (expanded from 13).',
'A **match** means two profiles share the same alleles at the tested loci. Analysts report the **random match probability**—the chance that an unrelated person would match by coincidence—often extremely small. DNA shows that biological material **could** have come from a person; it does not by itself show **when** or **how** it got there, so context matters.',
'**Exclusions**—profiles that do not match—are just as important, and DNA testing has helped exonerate wrongfully convicted people.',
"DNA also helps identify missing persons and victims of mass disasters by comparing remains with reference samples from relatives or personal items such as toothbrushes. Familial searching and genetic genealogy have expanded investigative uses but raise privacy and ethical questions.",
chk('What does a DNA match by itself show?',['That biological material could have come from that person','Exactly when the crime occurred','That the person committed the crime','The manner of death'],'Context determines meaning.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing a method',{a:{text:'An invented case provides only hair shafts without roots from a scene. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which analysis is most likely to succeed?',choices:[['Mitochondrial DNA analysis','b','Correct.',true],['Nuclear STR profiling','b','Shafts lack nuclear DNA.',false]]},b:{text:'The mtDNA matches a suspect. What limitation applies?',choices:[['Maternal relatives share the same mtDNA, so it cannot distinguish among them','c','Correct.',true],['None—mtDNA is unique to each person','c','mtDNA is shared maternally.',false]]},c:{text:'You report the result with its limitation and look for additional evidence.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['DNA is a double helix (A–T, G–C); forensic analysis targets short tandem repeats; sources include blood, saliva, semen, hair roots, skin, bone and teeth; prevent contamination with gloves, sterile tools and paper packaging.','STR profiling: extraction, quantitation, PCR amplification (denaturation, annealing, extension), capillary electrophoresis and an electropherogram of alleles (homozygous one peak, heterozygous two).','mtDNA is maternal, abundant and useful for hair shafts and old bones but less discriminating; Y-STR follows paternal lines and helps with mixtures; CODIS uses 20 core loci; matches are reported with random match probability, and exclusions matter.'],terms:[['Short tandem repeat','A short DNA sequence repeated a variable number of times.'],['PCR','Polymerase chain reaction, which copies targeted DNA.'],['CODIS','The FBI’s Combined DNA Index System.']],mcqs:[q('Which bases pair in DNA?','A with T, G with C','A with G, T with C','A with C, G with T','All pair equally','Base pairing.'),q('What does PCR do?','Makes many copies of targeted DNA','Separates DNA by size','Stains DNA','Destroys contamination','Amplification.'),q('Which technique separates STR fragments by size?','Capillary electrophoresis','PCR','Extraction','Luminol','Smaller move faster.'),q('Mitochondrial DNA is inherited from:','The mother','The father','Both parents equally','Neither parent','Maternal line.'),q('Y-STR analysis is especially useful for:','Male DNA in mixtures','Hair shafts','Maternal lines','Plant DNA','Y chromosome.'),q('How many core STR loci does CODIS use since 2017?','20','13','5','50','Expanded set.'),q('A heterozygous locus shows:','Two peaks','One peak','No peaks','Ten peaks','Different alleles.'),q('Biological evidence for DNA should be packaged in:','Paper after drying','Sealed plastic while wet','Open air','Metal cans','Prevents degradation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the steps of forensic STR DNA profiling and explain when mitochondrial or Y-STR analysis would be used instead.',rubric:[['Sources and contamination',['blood','saliva','skin','bone','contamination','gloves','paper']],['STR steps',['extraction','quantitation','pcr','denaturation','annealing','extension','capillary electrophoresis','electropherogram']],['Interpretation',['alleles','loci','profile','codis','20','random match','exclusion']],['Alternatives',['mitochondrial','mother','hair shaft','bone','y-str','male','mixture','paternal']]],model:'Forensic STR profiling begins with collecting biological evidence such as blood, saliva, semen, skin cells, hair roots, bone or teeth while preventing contamination with gloves, sterile swabs and dried paper packaging. In the laboratory, DNA is extracted from cells and purified, then quantitated to measure how much human DNA is present. Next, the polymerase chain reaction amplifies targeted short tandem repeat regions: heat separates the strands during denaturation, primers attach during annealing and DNA polymerase builds new strands during extension, and repeated cycles create millions of copies labeled with fluorescent tags. Capillary electrophoresis separates the fragments by size, and a laser detects them to produce an electropherogram, where peaks represent alleles; a person has two alleles at each locus, appearing as one peak if homozygous or two if heterozygous. The resulting profile across the 20 core loci used by CODIS can be compared with evidence or database profiles, and a match is reported with a random match probability, while an exclusion shows a person was not the source. Mitochondrial DNA analysis is used when nuclear DNA is unavailable or degraded—such as hair shafts without roots or old bones—because mtDNA has many copies per cell, but it is inherited from the mother and shared by maternal relatives, so it is less discriminating. Y-STR analysis targets the Y chromosome passed from father to son and is useful for detecting male DNA in mixtures with female DNA or tracing paternal lines, though male relatives share the same profile.'},sources:[RULES,NHGRI_STR,NHGRI_PCR,FBI_CODIS]};

export const FS10={title:'Forensic psychology and the Round Two written conclusion',hook:'Forensic psychologists and psychiatrists evaluate whether a defendant can stand trial, whether mental illness affected responsibility and whether someone is faking symptoms. That is 10% of the test. This lesson also brings everything together for Round Two: how to analyze a case in 40 minutes and write a conclusion that earns all 80 points.',objectives:['Explain competency, insanity, malingering and dissimulation.','Describe roles of forensic psychologists and psychiatrists, including profiling and eyewitness issues.','Analyze a case study systematically in 40 minutes.','Write a clear, evidence-based conclusion using the required template.'],sections:[
['Forensic psychology and psychiatry',[
'**Forensic psychology** applies psychology to legal questions; **forensic psychiatry** is a medical specialty that does the same and may prescribe treatment. Professionals evaluate defendants, assess risk of violence, testify as expert witnesses, consult on investigations and study eyewitness memory and interrogation.',
'**Competency to stand trial** asks whether a defendant **currently** understands the charges and proceedings and can assist their attorney (the U.S. **Dusky** standard). **Insanity** concerns the defendant’s mental state **at the time of the offense**. The **M’Naghten rule** asks whether, because of a mental disease, the person did not know the nature of the act or that it was wrong; other tests, such as the Model Penal Code standard, consider whether the person lacked substantial capacity to appreciate wrongfulness or conform conduct to the law.',
'Insanity is a legal, not medical, term, and successful insanity defenses are rare.',
sort('Competency or insanity?',['Competency to stand trial','Insanity'],[['Can the defendant understand the court process now?',0],['Did the defendant know the act was wrong at the time?',1],['Can the defendant assist their attorney?',0],['M’Naghten rule',1],['Dusky standard',0]])]],
['Malingering, dissimulation and assessment',[
'**Malingering** is the intentional **feigning or exaggeration** of symptoms for an external benefit, such as avoiding trial or obtaining compensation. **Dissimulation** is the conscious and deliberate attempt to **minimize or deny** symptoms of a mental disorder—for example, to avoid hospitalization or appear competent. Both are assessed with interviews, records, observation and psychological tests designed to detect inconsistent responses.',
'Forensic evaluators also assess **risk of violence**, **psychopathy** (for example, with structured checklists) and **substance use**, always aware that evaluations have legal consequences and must be objective.',
'The difference between malingering and dissimulation is a classic test question.',
match('Term and meaning',[['Malingering','Faking or exaggerating symptoms for gain'],['Dissimulation','Minimizing or denying symptoms'],['Competency','Current ability to understand and assist in trial'],['Insanity','Mental state at the time of the offense'],['M’Naghten rule','Did not know the nature of the act or that it was wrong'],['Expert witness','Testifies about specialized knowledge']])]],
['Profiling, eyewitnesses and interrogation',[
'**Criminal profiling** infers characteristics of an unknown offender from crime scene behavior and evidence; it can guide investigations but is not proof and has limited scientific support for some approaches. **Eyewitness memory** is reconstructive and can be influenced by stress, weapon focus, lighting, time delays, leading questions and **cross-race effects**; mistaken eyewitness identification has contributed to many wrongful convictions.',
'**False confessions** can result from coercive interrogation, fatigue, youth or cognitive impairment. Best practices include recording interrogations, double-blind lineups and careful questioning.',
'These issues remind investigators to rely on multiple lines of evidence.',
sort('Factor that reduces eyewitness accuracy?',['Reduces accuracy','Improves reliability'],[['Weapon focus',0],['Double-blind lineup procedures',1],['Long delay before identification',0],['Leading questions',0],['Recording confidence immediately',1],['High stress',0]])]],
['A 40-minute analysis plan',[
'In Round Two, teams have **40 minutes** to analyze and write, then **5 minutes** to upload a **PDF**. A sample plan: **0–10 minutes**—both members read all materials (scene, police report, autopsy), highlighting times, temperatures, injuries, toxicology and stomach contents. **10–18**—build a **timeline** and list evidence for each conclusion. **18–22**—agree on time of death range, cause, manner and contributing conditions. **22–37**—type the conclusion using the template. **37–40**—proofread, save and export to PDF.',
'Divide work: one member types while the other organizes evidence and checks facts, then switch for proofreading. Notes and provided documents may be written on but must be turned in at the end. No internet research is allowed—use your knowledge and the case facts.',
'Save frequently; electricity is not provided, so start with a fully charged device.',
order('Round Two analysis plan',['Read every document and highlight key facts','Build a timeline','List evidence for each conclusion','Agree on time, cause, manner and contributing conditions','Type the conclusion in the template','Proofread, export to PDF and upload'])]],
['Writing the conclusion',[
'Use the required template: **Time of Death Range**, **Immediate Cause of Death**, **Manner of Death**, **Other conditions contributing to the immediate cause of death** (if applicable) and **Evidence to support opinion**. Be specific: “Between 10:00 p.m. on March 3 and 2:00 a.m. on March 4,” “Gunshot wound to the chest perforating the heart,” “Homicide.”',
'In the evidence section, organize by conclusion. For each, list **numerous specific facts** and explain them with anatomy, physiology and forensic science: “Body temperature of 89.6 °F at 6:00 a.m., using a cooling rate of about 1.5 °F per hour, indicates death about six hours earlier; fixed lividity on the back and full rigor are consistent.” Write clearly and logically, and proofread—grammar and spelling are worth 10 points.',
'Avoid claims the evidence does not support; explain uncertainty where appropriate.',
ex('Evidence paragraph structure','Write evidence for a manner of death conclusion (invented case).',['State the conclusion: The manner of death is homicide.','Fact 1: The entrance wound on the back of the head shows no soot or stippling, indicating distant fire.','Science: A distant wound in a location the decedent could not easily reach is inconsistent with suicide.','Fact 2: No firearm was found at the scene.','Fact 3: Defensive injuries were noted on the forearms.','Link: Together, these facts support homicide rather than suicide or accident.'],'Each conclusion is supported by several specific facts and scientific reasoning (invented for practice).')]],
['Common mistakes and how to avoid them',[
'Common mistakes include **confusing cause and manner** (“Manner: gunshot wound”), listing **contributing conditions** as the cause, giving a time of death that ignores some evidence, **failing to cite specific facts**, writing vague evidence (“the autopsy shows it”), forgetting to **export as PDF** and running out of time to proofread.',
'Prevent them by using the template headings, checking that manner is one of the five categories, citing at least three facts per conclusion and setting a firm time to stop writing and proofread.',
'Practice with sample cases under timed conditions, scoring with the rubric.',
sort('Correct or mistaken entry?',['Correct','Mistaken'],[['Manner: Homicide',0],['Manner: Gunshot wound',1],['Cause: Acute fentanyl toxicity',0],['Contributing condition listed as the cause',1],['Evidence cites body temperature with calculation',0],['Evidence: “The autopsy proves it”',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Writing under time',{a:{text:'With 8 minutes left, your team has written the cause and manner but not the evidence section. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Write concise bullet-style evidence for each conclusion, then proofread and export','b','Correct.',true],['Keep polishing the opening sentences','b','Evidence items are worth the most points.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate wrote “Manner: Overdose.” How do you fix it?',choices:[['Manner: Accident (if evidence supports), with overdose as the cause','c','Correct. Manner is one of five categories.',true],['Leave it','c','Overdose is a cause, not a manner.',false]]},c:{text:'You export the PDF and upload within the five-minute window.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Competency concerns current ability to understand and assist at trial (Dusky); insanity concerns mental state at the time of the offense (M’Naghten); malingering fakes or exaggerates symptoms, while dissimulation deliberately minimizes or denies them.','Profiling guides but does not prove; eyewitness memory is affected by stress, weapon focus, delays, leading questions and cross-race effects; false confessions can occur.','Round Two: read and highlight, build a timeline, agree on conclusions and type the template (time range, immediate cause, manner from the five categories, contributing conditions, evidence) with several specific, scientifically explained facts per conclusion; proofread and export a PDF.'],terms:[['Malingering','Intentionally faking or exaggerating symptoms for gain.'],['Dissimulation','Deliberately minimizing or denying symptoms of a mental disorder.'],['Competency to stand trial','A defendant’s current ability to understand proceedings and assist counsel.']],mcqs:[q('What is the term for a conscious and deliberate attempt to minimize or deny symptoms of a mental disorder?','Dissimulation','Deception','Malingering','Incompetence','HOSA sample question (Bell).'),q('Faking symptoms for external gain is:','Malingering','Dissimulation','Competency','Insanity','Exaggeration.'),q('Competency to stand trial concerns:','Current ability to understand and assist','Mental state at the time of the crime','Guilt','Sentencing','Dusky.'),q('The M’Naghten rule concerns:','Knowing the nature of the act or that it was wrong','Competency only','DNA evidence','Fingerprints','Insanity test.'),q('Which reduces eyewitness accuracy?','Weapon focus','Double-blind lineups','Immediate confidence recording','Good lighting','Attention on the weapon.'),q('Which is a valid manner of death entry?','Accident','Overdose','Gunshot wound','Heart attack','Five categories.'),q('How much time do teams have to analyze and write?','40 minutes','15 minutes','60 minutes','90 minutes','Plus 5 to upload.'),q('In what format must the conclusion be uploaded?','PDF','Word only','Handwritten','Image','Single PDF.')],frq:{prompt:'Using an invented case, write the five sections of a Round Two conclusion and explain how your evidence section connects facts to each conclusion.',rubric:[['Template sections',['time of death','immediate cause','manner','contributing','evidence']],['Specific facts',['temperature','rigor','livor','stomach','toxicology','wound','scene']],['Scientific reasoning',['1.5','cooling','fixed','range','mechanism','physiology']],['Accuracy and clarity',['five categories','cause vs manner','logical','proofread','pdf']]],model:'Invented case conclusion. Time of Death Range: between 10:00 p.m. on March 3 and 1:00 a.m. on March 4. Immediate Cause of Death: acute mixed drug toxicity (fentanyl and alcohol) causing respiratory depression. Manner of Death: Accident. Other conditions contributing to the immediate cause of death: coronary artery disease and obesity. Evidence to support opinion: The body was found at 7:00 a.m. with a rectal temperature of 89.6 °F in a 70 °F room; using a cooling rate of about 1.5 °F per hour, (98.6 − 89.6) ÷ 1.5 ≈ 6 hours, placing death around 1:00 a.m., and full rigor with lividity fixed on the back is consistent with roughly 6–9 hours. A friend reported last seeing the decedent alive at 10:00 p.m., and a partially digested meal in the stomach suggests death within a few hours of a dinner eaten at 9:00 p.m. Toxicology from femoral blood confirmed fentanyl at a concentration associated with fatal toxicity and a blood alcohol concentration of 0.12%, and opioids combined with alcohol depress breathing; frothy fluid in the airways and heavy lungs are consistent with respiratory depression. No injuries, defensive wounds or signs of forced entry were found, and a used syringe and prescription bottles were present, supporting an accidental overdose rather than suicide or homicide; no note or history of intent was reported. Severe narrowing of the coronary arteries and an enlarged heart likely reduced tolerance of low oxygen and are listed as contributing conditions.'},sources:[RULES,APA_FORENSIC,NIJ]};
